\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The counting product leaves the limit open}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{The cubical torus of \ref{fdip-00OK} carries the Whitney map
of \ref{fdip-00SC}. The cochain-map identity of the simplicial
comparison therefore holds for these cubes. What remains open
is the comparison of the counting product with the
#{\mathrm L^2} product of these lowest-order cubical Whitney
forms. The counting product is the diagonal product of
\ref{fdip-00S5}. The #{\mathrm L^2} product of these forms
is a different pairing. The comparison of the two pairings,
and the convergence of the counting Hodge theory, remain
open.}

\p{Arnold, Boffi and Bonizzoni state an approximation theorem
for their spaces under its own conditions
\citet{Theorem 6.1, p. 14}{arnoldboffibonizzoni2014cubic}.
The estimates are
\citet{(15)–(17), p. 12}{arnoldboffibonizzoni2014cubic}.
For affine element maps the reference space contains every
polynomial #{p}-form of their degree #{r}. For multilinear
element maps it contains their #{Q_{r+p}^{-}\Lambda^p}. Both
#{\Lambda} and #{r} are their symbols. Neither is matched
here to the lowest-order family. The theorem
is not a convergence result for the counting product, and it
is not a mass-lumping theorem.}

\p{The scaling in \ref{fdip-00S5} is an identity of finite
pairings. It does not supply the missing limit.}
